AU  - Paton, D.M.
TI  - Bempedoic acid: ATP-citrate lyase inhibitor AMPK activator Treatment of hypercholesterolemia
PY  - 2017
T2  - Drugs of the Future
VL  - 42
IS  - 4
SP  - 201
EP  - 208
DO  - 10.1358/dof.2017.042.04.2530248
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85018929048&doi=10.1358%2fdof.2017.042.04.2530248&partnerID=40&md5=a114d0a60e463c0d10fa7065d6176724
AB  - Bempedoic acid (ETC-1002) is being developed for the treatment of hypercholesterolemia. It has two mechanisms of action: ETC-1002 is converted in the liver to ETC-1002-coenzyme A (CoA), which in turn inhibits ATP-citrate lyase (ACL) and activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK). ETC-1002 is thus a prodrug as the free acid does not inhibit ACL or activate AMPK. ACL inhibits the biosynthesis of cholesterol at an earlier step than the statins. Importantly, ETC-1002 does not inhibit the synthesis of cholesterol in skeletal muscle, because it is not converted to ETC-1002-CoA in this tissue. It also has anti-inflammatory actions as well as the ability to improve glucose tolerance. In clinical trials it has consistently reduced LDL-cholesterol levels in patients with hypercholesterolemia. This effect has also been shown to occur in patients with type 2 diabetes but without causing any further impairment in their glucose tolerance. The drug also reduces LDL-cholesterol in patients already taking a statin and in patients with a history of statin intolerance, but without causing significant skeletal muscle adverse events such as myalgia. ETC-1002 is now being studied to determine its long-term effects on cardiovascular morbidity and mortality. © 2017 Clarivate Analytics.
